NEW_CORR = ('<span class="rev">▼ What happened in October–early November.</span> Yemeni government forces, backed by Saudi air strikes, retook Dhubab and the Mokha area on 5–6/10, so the coast around Bab el-Mandeb is now contested rather than held outright by the Houthis; the Houthis still declare an embargo on Saudi ships, but no ship has been confirmed hit since 24/08. Attacks moved to the Gulf coast and the capital: a projectile struck inside Yanbu port on 01/10; a missile aimed at Yanbu was intercepted on 17/10 and a tanker was hit off Jubail the same day; the Houthis claimed strikes on <b>Ras Tanura</b> on 27/10 (a fire near the terminal was contained; Aramco did not confirm), on Rabigh and Abqaiq on 29–30/10, and on Riyadh on 6–7/11, where drones and a cruise missile were intercepted. Aramco says its facilities were attacked in the quarter and afterwards, with no material impact. The East–West pipeline ran at reduced throughput all period with no new outage. Exports recovered: tanker trackers put October at <b>6.5–7.9 million barrels a day</b> (near pre-war), with about 2.5 million a day from Yanbu. Brent averaged about <b>$108 in October</b>, peaking at $117.6 on 30/10 after a mine strike in Hormuz, then fell to about <b>$102 by 06/11</b> as US–Iran mediation through Qatar, Pakistan and Oman showed progress — even as the US carried out limited strikes on Iranian air defences on 6–7/11. OPEC+ kept targets unchanged on 04/10 and 01/11 (Saudi quota 10.5 million barrels a day); it meets again on 29/11.'
            '\n      <br><br><span class="corr">⚠ How our estimate changes.</span> Version 5 expected SAR 245–280 billion for the year. The July–September report changes the arithmetic: January–September is already <b>SAR 244.3 billion</b> (official). For the Ministry\'s SAR 245 billion to hold, October–December would have to end in balance — and no quarter has since 2022; the same months cost about SAR 154 billion in 2025. October\'s export recovery helps, but oil sales reach the budget about three months after the barrels ship, so most of it lands in 2027. Our most likely range is now <b>SAR 300–350 billion (about 6.0–7.0% of the size of the economy)</b>; a calmer path still ends above the Ministry\'s figure because nine months are already in; losing the convoys again would push the year above SAR 360 billion. All of this is EH arithmetic on the Ministry\'s figures, not a Ministry forecast.')
